    "acquired_at": "2026-11-24T20:29:39.026140+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-24T20:29:39.026140+00:00",
    "body": "Hyundai’s new IONIQ 3 checks all the boxes as an affordable EV: it offers over 300 miles of range, a practical interior, and Hyundai’s new Pleos infotainment system. So, will Hyundai bring it to the US?\nIs Hyundai launching the IONIQ 3 in the US?\nThe Hyundai IONIQ 3 is now on sale across Europe and the UK as a smaller sibling to the IONIQ 5, positioned between the Inster EV and Hyundai Kona Electric.\nAlthough it’s part of the IONIQ sub-brand, the IONIQ 3 features a new “Aero Hatch” design. The new look “reimagines the compact EV silhouette,” Hyundai said, designed to optimize efficiency and interior space.\nThe response so far has been “extraordinary,” Ashley Andrew, president of Hyundai Motor UK, said after launching the IONIQ 3 last month.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nAccording to Andrew, the compact EV received “more customers registering their interest before launch than any new Hyundai we’ve introduced in the UK,” and that’s including gas-powered and electric vehicles.\nThe first reviews of the IONIQ 3 have been overwhelmingly positive, highlighting its efficiency, practical interior, and affordable price tag.\nView this post on Instagram A post shared by KindelAuto (@kindelauto)\nSo why doesn’t Hyundai sell it in the US? The IONIQ 3 was recently captured driving in public in the US, raising speculation, or perhaps hope, that it will come to the States after all.\nThe model spotted in the images from KindelAuto is the IONIQ 3 N Line, a sportier, higher-priced trim.\nWhile it’s among the first sightings in the US, we have seen several Hyundai Motor Group electric vehicles testing in the US that are still not available and likely won’t ever be, such as Kia’s EV5 SUV and PV5 van.\nHyundai IONIQ 3 N-Line (Source: Hyundai UK)\nThe IONIQ 3 is built at Hyundai Motor’s Izmit, Turkey, plant and is specifically designed for European drivers.\nWe’ve reached out to Hyundai to see if anything has changed, but we haven’t heard back yet. We’ll update you when we hear more.\nIn Europe and the UK, Hyundai offers the IONIQ 3 with two battery packs: 42 kWh and 61 kWh. The base IONIQ 3 Advance trim with the 42 kWh battery starts at £18,495 ($25,000) OTR and offers up to 213 miles of WLTP range. That’s including the UK’s £3,750 Electric Car Grant.\nHyundai IONIQ 3 interior (Source: Hyundai)\nThe IONIQ 3 Advance with the 61 kWh battery starts at £21,995 ($29,000) and offers a “class-leading” WLTP range of up to 308 miles. That also includes the UK government ECG grant.\nMeasuring 4,155 mm (163.5″) long, 1,800 mm (70.9″) wide, and 1,505 mm (59.3″) high, with a wheelbase of 2,680 mm (105.5″), Hyundai’s electric hatch is even smaller than the Kia EV3 and Volkswagen ID.3.\nThe IONIQ 3 is also Hyundai’s first vehicle in Europe equipped with its new Pleos Connect infotainment system.\nHyundai IONIQ 3 interior with Pleos Connect infotainment system (Source: Hyundai)\nHyundai’s new system is more like using a smartphone. It’s based on the Android Automotive OS with apps and customizable screens, similar to Tesla models. It’s available in the IONIQ 3 with a 12.9″ or 14.6″ center infotainment screen, plus an optional driver display.\nUnlike the larger IONIQ 5, the IONIQ 3 uses a 400V architecture. However, the 42 kWh battery can still recharge from 10% to 80% in about 29 minutes. The 61 kWh battery takes around 30 minutes.\nIn Germany and other European markets, Hyundai’s compact IONIQ 3 starts at about €28,950 ($33,000).\nHyundai IONIQ 3 (Source: Hyundai UK)\nWhile Hyundai currently has no plans to bring the IONIQ 3 to the US, it does sell the IONIQ 5, built at the Hyundai Motor Group Metaplant America outside Savannah, GA, alongside the three-row IONIQ 9.\nStarting at about $35,000, Hyundai’s IONIQ 5 has remained among the top-selling electric vehicles in the US outside of the Tesla Model Y and Model 3.\nIn fact, Hyundai was just named the “Best Electric Vehicle Brand” by US News & World Report for the first time for 2027.\nHyundai’s sister company, Kia, also launched the 2027 EV3, which is now on sale starting at just $29,890 in the US.\nWant to take a closer look at one? We’ve made it easy for you. You can use the links below to find available Hyundai IONIQ 5 , IONIQ 9, and Kia EV3 models near you.\nHyundai IONIQ 5 — See offers\nHyundai IONIQ 9 — See offers\nKia EV3 — See offers\nSource: KindelAut o\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
